# ghcacher.dl6 — v5 gh-cache.dl (examples/gh-cache.dl:1-141) re-expressed in the v6 dl
# surface. Extraction-lab discipline: zero engine/grammar changes. Every point where
# the v6 surface cannot say what v5 said is called out inline AND recorded, with
# file:line evidence, in ghcacher-findings.md (read that file for the full grading).
#
# THREE STRUCTURAL GAPS this file works around, all load-bearing for the shape below
# (full detail + evidence in findings.md):
#
#   1. No @async / @next in v6/dl/grammar/dl.langium (grepped, zero matches). A
#      call resolving to a `sh` signature is v6's effect-firing surface: `host(args)` demands a
#      content-addressed request, and HostRunner (v6/dl/src/1_hosts.ts:357-523) answers
#      it out of band. No separate `@async` marker exists or is needed — every probe is
#      async by construction. This part maps cleanly onto salt_minting=content_addressed.
#
#   2. No @next carry: a rule cannot read "what I derived last tick." v5's etag/etag_next
#      split exists to freeze etag at tick-start so `poll`'s bootstrap negation
#      (`!etag(ep,_)`) never closes a cycle back through resp -> poll. Ported literally
#      (negation instead of @next-freeze), that same shape is a NEGATION EDGE inside a
#      cycle (poll -[neg]-> etag -> resp -> poll), which v6's stratify() rejects at load
#      (0_ast_bridge.ts:948-951, diag code "non-stratifiable" — reproduced empirically,
#      see findings.md). Worked around here by dropping the negation entirely: `etag`
#      is the UNION of an always-true bootstrap fact and the resp-derived value, which
#      is ordinary positive recursion (stratifiable, ships in conformance.dl6 as
#      proves_recursion). Net effect: `poll(ep, "")` never goes away, so the empty-etag
#      identity keeps getting demanded every tick — harmless (content-addressed cache
#      hit, no-op) but not what v5's negation intended, and NOT a general substitute for
#      @next (see findings.md's cadence finding for the case this does NOT cover).
#
#   3. No clock/tick builtin: v6/dl/src/5_diag.ts:48-56 is the WHOLE builtin rel surface
#      (file/node/edge/sig/site/const/span_line/diag) — no `clock(secs, bucket)`, no
#      wall-clock anything, and v6/dl/src/main.ts has no setInterval/scheduler at all.
#      There is nothing in the language OR the runtime that changes on its own with
#      wall-clock time. gh-cache.dl's whole rate/cadence mechanic (clock bucket salts
#      the request id so an unchanged resource still gets re-checked every N seconds)
#      has no v6 spelling. This file's `poll` therefore only re-fires when `etag`
#      actually changes (a resource CHANGE re-triggers exactly one more fetch); an
#      UNCHANGED resource is never re-checked, ever. This is SLOT-SWR's answer for this
#      port: see findings.md for the two candidate spellings and why the in-language one
#      is what is below.
#
# --- what to poll --------------------------------------------------------------
use http.

rel watch(ep: text).
watch("repos/cli/cli").

# --- the conditional request (the cache) ---------------------------------------
# Same shell body as v5 (examples/gh-cache.dl:75-80). The host declaration
# separates request inputs from response outputs, and the template references every
# declared input and no output.
rel get(url: text, headers: text, prev_etag: text, bucket: int)
  -> (status: int, response_headers: json, body: json, bytes: int).

# etag: bootstrap fact UNION resp-derived value (gap #2 above). Deliberately NOT
# `rel(1)`: retention markers only govern EDB-style commit() writes (applyRelWrite,
# v6/dl/src/3_runtime.ts:420-490); a rule-headed rel like this one is recomputed and
# diffed every tick regardless of its retention marker (diffAgainstTables,
# 3_runtime.ts:574-589, checks no retention at all) — `rel(1)` here would be a silent
# no-op AND, if it ever did apply, its "keep newest only" sweep is a GLOBAL sweep of
# the whole table (3_runtime.ts:457-469), not a per-`ep` upsert, which would be actively
# wrong for more than one watched endpoint. See findings.md's retention finding.
rel etag(ep: text, tag: text).
etag(ep, "") <- watch(ep).
etag(ep, tag) <- resp(ep, 200, tag, _).

rel poll(ep: text, prev: text, headers: text).
poll(ep, prev, headers) <-
  watch(ep), etag(ep, prev),
  headers := concat(["{\"If-None-Match\":\"", prev, "\"}"]).

# Fire it. Content-addressed on (fetch, ep, prev) — the host call is the async effect;
# there is no separate @async marker to write. Every status lands (not just 200): the
# template always emits status/tag/body, matching v5's raw `resp` (examples/gh-cache.dl:
# 89-90) before its 200-only filter.
rel arrival(ep: text, status: int, response_headers: json, body: json).
arrival(ep, status, response_headers, body) <-
  poll(ep, prev, headers),
  get(ep, headers, prev, 0, status, response_headers, body, _bytes).

rel resp(ep: text, status: int, tag: text, body: json).
resp(ep, status, tag, body) <-
  arrival(ep, status, response_headers, body),
  decode(response_headers, {etag: tag: text}).

# --- normalize: entities from 200 bodies ----------------------------------------
# v5's jsonp/json term ops are `decode` here, in-process and with no subprocess:
# the two gaps this section used to name are both closed.
rel stars(ep: text, n: text).
stars(ep, n) <- resp(ep, 200, _, body), decode(body, {stargazers_count: n: text}).

rel full_name(ep: text, name: text).
full_name(ep, name) <- resp(ep, 200, _, body), decode(body, {full_name: name: text}).

# v5's pull_request (examples/gh-cache.dl:120-124) needs an ARRAY explode with
# correlated nested fields; `decode(body, [... {...}])` is that, so this is the
# spelling rather than a named absence.
rel pull_request(ep: text, number: int, title: text, head_ref: text).
pull_request(ep, number, title, head_ref) <-
  resp(ep, 200, _, body),
  decode(body, [... {number: number: int, title: title: text,
                     head: {ref: head_ref: text}}]).

# --- the change feed (append-only) -----------------------------------------------
# v5 needs an explicit @next carry (examples/gh-cache.dl:132-137) because a PLAIN
# derived rule in v5 (like in v6) is recomputed from current support every tick, so an
# entity that stops being currently derivable would otherwise vanish from the log. No
# @next exists in v6, so this is written as a plain derived rule instead, relying on a
# DIFFERENT property: `resp`'s identity is content-addressed on (ep, prev), prev is
# itself a value that only ever advances forward (etag never repeats an old tag once a
# newer one is known — gap #3 above already caps how far it advances), and zero salts
# are used on the fetch probe, so a landed `__resp_fetch` row is NEVER superseded
# (1_hosts.ts:86, "absent any salt_N key... identityDigest === fullDigest, no
# supersession ever fires") and therefore never disappears. `stars`/`full_name` inherit
# that permanence through the join, and `change_log` inherits it from them, with
# ordinary rel set-semantics (2_schema.ts:16-17) doing the (ep,kind,val) dedup v5's
# accumulator did explicitly. This is an EMPIRICALLY-REASONED substitute, not a general
# @next replacement — it only works because the probe's identity is keyed on values
# that strictly advance and nothing here uses a salt. See findings.md's accumulate
# finding for what would break this (a salted probe, or a `rel(1)` in the chain).
rel change_log(ep: text, kind: text, val: text).
change_log(ep, "stars", n) <- stars(ep, n).
change_log(ep, "full_name", v) <- full_name(ep, v).

# --- read the cache ---------------------------------------------------------------
# No `effect_log` builtin exists in v6 (5_diag.ts's builtin list has no such rel); the
# closest visibility into effect state is GET /idb/__resp_fetch (a minted EDB rel) or
# the effect_cache table directly (not surfaced as a queryable rel at all). Query lines
# below cover what v6 CAN show.
? change_log(ep, kind, val).
? etag(ep, tag).
